{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.72,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["J5", "U1", "U4", "J2"],
      "pins": ["J5.3", "U1.1", "U4.1", "J2.6"],
      "nets": ["NET0003", "GND"],
      "observed": "The potentiometer J5 pin 3 is wired to GND and simultaneously carries the NET0003 label. NET0003 also connects to U1 pin 1 (RESET/PB5), U4 pin 1 (RESET/PB5), and J2 pin 6 (RST) of the ISP header. Thus the MCU reset line is tied directly to ground.",
      "expected": "Pin 3 of the pot should be GND only; the RESET/RST net (NET0003) must remain a separate node so the MCU can run and the programmer can drive reset.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "J5 POT: 1→+5V, 2→NET0010, 3→GND with NET0003 on the same wire at pin 3 (sheet /)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "U1 pin 1 → NET0003; U4 pin 1 → NET0003; J2 pin 6 RST → NET0003"}
      ],
      "impact": "MCU held in permanent reset — board does not boot or respond to programming.",
      "confidence": 0.7
    },
    {
      "category": "short_to_ground",
      "components": ["Q1", "Q2", "J9"],
      "pins": ["Q1.3", "Q2.3", "J9.2"],
      "nets": ["VCC_OUT", "NET0005"],
      "observed": "Q1 (BC817) collector (pin 3) is wired to Q2 drain (pin 3), which is the switched fan-supply node VCC_OUT feeding J9 pin 2; the same node also carries NET0005 (the TACH line at J9 pin 3). Q1's emitter is GND and its base is driven from NET0006 via R5.",
      "expected": "In a high-side P-FET driver the NPN collector belongs on Q2's gate (pin 1); the fan supply (VCC_OUT) and the TACH feedback (NET0005) must be distinct nets, and no transistor should short the supply rail to ground.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Q1 BC817: 1(B)→R5→NET0006, 2(E)→GND, 3(C)→Q2.3(D); Q2.2(S)→VCC; J9 FAN: 2→VCC_OUT, 3→NET0005"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "an ATtiny-driven fan controller"}
      ],
      "impact": "Driving NET0006 low/high shorts the regulated fan rail to ground through Q1 and merges the PWM/feedback path into the supply; fan cannot be powered or speed-controlled correctly.",
      "confidence": 0.55
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["U1", "U4"],
      "pins": ["U1.2", "U1.3", "U4.2", "U4.3"],
      "nets": ["NET0008", "NET0010"],
      "observed": "The two alternative MCU footprints disagree on PB3/PB4: U1 (ATtiny45) maps PB3(pin2)→NET0010 and PB4(pin3)→NET0008, while U4 (ATtiny13V) maps PB4(pin3)→NET0010 and PB3(pin2)→NET0008.",
      "expected": "Interchangeable THT/SMD footprints must present an identical pin-to-net map so one layout/firmware works for either part.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "U1 right side: 2→NET0010, 3→NET0008; U4 right side: 3→NET0010, 2→NET0008"}
      ],
      "impact": "Whichever footprint is populated gets the pot-sense and clamped signals on opposite GPIOs, breaking firmware portability between the two builds.",
      "confidence": 0.5
    }
  ],
  "summary": "An anomaly is present: the RESET/RST net (NET0003) is merged with GND at the potentiometer's third terminal, holding the MCU in permanent reset, and the fan driver shows further deviations (NPN collector on the P-FET drain/supply node rather than its gate, and inconsistent PB3/PB4 mapping between the two MCU footprints)."
}

